Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.10.2.10", "inferred_role": "domain_controller_logs_cleared", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T02:51:18+0200", "last_seen": "2027-10-04T03:22:01+0200", "agent_name": "DC-CORP-01"},
    {"ip": "10.10.7.41", "inferred_role": "workstation_source_of_rogue_account_first_use", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T03:18:44+0200", "last_seen": "2027-10-04T03:18:44+0200", "agent_name": "WS-HELPDESK-12"},
    {"ip": "10.10.x.x", "inferred_role": "member_file_server_lateral_target", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T03:22:01+0200", "last_seen": "2027-10-04T03:22:01+0200", "agent_name": "FILESVR-FIN-02"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence / Privilege Escalation (Account Manipulation)", "technique": "T1136.002 + T1098", "what_happened": "At 2027-10-04 02:51:18 CEST, Windows Security Event 4720 'A user account was created' fired on DC-CORP-01 (10.10.2.10): TargetUserName=it.helpdesk2, SubjectUserName=administrator, SubjectDomainName=CORP. The built-in 'administrator' was used to create a new domain account named to mimic the IT helpdesk naming convention. At 02:53:42 (~2.5 min later), Event 4732 'A member was added to a security-enabled local group' fired on the same DC: TargetUserName=it.helpdesk2, MemberName=CN=it.helpdesk2,OU=Users,DC=corp,DC=local, GroupName=Domain Admins. The rogue account was elevated to Domain Admin within ~2.5 minutes of creation — the operational compression and the masquerading naming pattern (looks like a normal IT helpdesk user) is the signature of attacker tradecraft, not an admin onboarding workflow.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4720 (account create) + Event 4732 (group add Domain Admins) under same SubjectUserName=administrator within 2.5 minutes. Diagnostic of credential-access-followed-by-persistence playbook.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T02:51:18+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / Verification Recon", "technique": "T1069.002", "what_happened": "At 02:58:33, Sysmon Event 1 on DC-CORP-01 fired: image=net.exe + commandLine='net group \"Domain Admins\" /domain' + parentImage=cmd.exe + user=CORP\\administrator. The attacker verified their own privilege grant — confirming 'it.helpdesk2' is now visible in the Domain Admins group output. This 'verify-after-add' is the universal attacker tradecraft after T1136+T1098 to make sure the persistence worked.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: net.exe with cmdline 'net group \"Domain Admins\" /domain' from CORP\\administrator on DC-CORP-01, within 5 min after the 4732 group-add. Verification recon signature.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T02:58:33+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Indicator Removal (Anti-Forensics)", "technique": "T1070.001", "what_happened": "At 03:02:08, Sysmon Event 1 on DC-CORP-01 fired: image=wevtutil.exe + commandLine='wevtutil.exe cl Security' + parentImage=cmd.exe + user=CORP\\svc-admin. NOTE the user pivot: the account creating + verifying (administrator) DIFFERS from the account clearing logs (svc-admin). Either the attacker is using a hijacked service account to compartmentalize, OR svc-admin is a separate compromised principal. The 'wevtutil.exe cl Security' is the canonical Microsoft tool to clear the Windows Security event log — exactly the anti-forensics action MITRE catalogs as T1070.001.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=wevtutil.exe + commandLine contains 'cl Security' on DC-CORP-01 by CORP\\svc-admin, ~9 min after the 4732 group add. The cl Security operation is THE indicator-removal technique.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T03:02:08+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Indicator Removal (Cleared)", "technique": "T1070.001", "what_happened": "At 03:12:19 CEST, Windows Security Event 1102 'The audit log was cleared' fired on DC-CORP-01: SubjectUserName=svc-admin, SubjectDomainName=CORP, SubjectLogonId=0x3e7. Event 1102 is a CRITICAL Microsoft design choice: it is ALWAYS written even when the audit log is cleared — the log-clear cannot hide itself. This is the trigger event for the entire investigation: the attacker tried to erase their tracks, but the act of erasure is itself logged and forwarded to Wazuh. Around the same time (03:12:35), Wazuh internal monitoring reported INGESTION_DROP momentarily then resumed on agent DC-CORP-01 — the log buffer flush correlated to the clear is detectable from the SIEM telemetry side as well.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 1102 (rule for audit-log-cleared, level 12 or higher) with SubjectUserName=svc-admin on DC-CORP-01. Wazuh INGESTION_DROP from agent DC-CORP-01 at +16 seconds confirms the local log buffer was flushed.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T03:12:19+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Lateral Movement (First Use of Rogue Account)", "technique": "T1078.002 + T1021", "what_happened": "At 03:18:44 CEST (~6 minutes AFTER the log clear), Windows Security Event 4624 fired on DC-CORP-01: targetUserName=it.helpdesk2, logonType=3 (Network), ipAddress=10.10.7.41 (workstation WS-HELPDESK-12). The newly-created rogue Domain Admin account is now in active use, authenticating from an end-user workstation. The post-clear timing is operationally important: the attacker likely BELIEVES they erased the 4720 + 4732 evidence, and is now operating under the rogue identity assuming the trail is cold. They do not know that (a) Event 1102 is ALWAYS logged, and (b) the 4720 + 4732 events were forwarded to Wazuh BEFORE the clear and are recoverable from wazuh-alerts.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 logonType=3 with targetUserName=it.helpdesk2 + ipAddress=10.10.7.41 on DC-CORP-01, ~6 min after the 1102 clear. First use of the rogue account = the persistence-payoff phase.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T03:18:44+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Lateral Movement (Internal Pivot)", "technique": "T1021", "what_happened": "At 03:22:01 CEST, Windows Security Event 4624 fired on FILESVR-FIN-02: targetUserName=CORP\\it.helpdesk2, logonType=3 (Network), source DC-CORP-01. The rogue Domain Admin is being used to test lateral access to a finance file server — the attacker is mapping the blast-radius of their Domain Admin grant. Finance file shares are a typical pre-data-exfiltration reconnaissance target.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 logonType=3 with targetUserName=CORP\\it.helpdesk2 on FILESVR-FIN-02 sourced from DC-CORP-01 — lateral test by the rogue account ~3 minutes after first use.", "first_seen": "2027-10-04T03:22:01+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-10-04T02:51:18+0200", "end": "2027-10-04T03:22:01+0200"},
